<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karaikal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karaikal is a coastal town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel Coast</a> of south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in the Kaveri delta. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> acquired it from the Raja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> in 1739 and governed it as part of French India until its de facto transfer to India in 1954, completed legally in 1962. It forms a district of the Union Territory of Puducherry, although it is surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karaikal.jpg" alt="Karaikal" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P Jeganathan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karaikal_Beach_JEG2443.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Karaikal is a port city and the administrative headquarters of Karaikal District, the second-largest city in the Union Territory of Puducherry after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a>. It lies at the centre of a small coastal enclave, bounded on the north by Mayiladuthurai district, on the south by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagapattinam">Nagapattinam</a> district and on the west by Tiruvarur district, all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, and on the east by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. The enclave is flat, covered by a thick mantle of alluvium and by the distributaries of the Kaveri, whose branches, among them the Arasalar, supply its irrigation.</p><p>The origin of the name is uncertain. The Imperial Gazetteer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> gives it as fish pass, and another suggestion is a canal built of lime mix, though no trace of such a canal survives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1738 Sayaji, the exiled Rajah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, promised Karaikal to the French in exchange for their help. The transfer took place only in 1739, with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a>. In 1749 Pratap Singh ceded 81 more villages to the French during their siege of Thanjavur, a grant confirmed by treaty in 1754. The town changed hands several times in the conflicts between the French and the British. On 5 April 1760 British forces captured it after a ten-day defence, defeating the governor Pierre Renault.</p><p>Karaikal was restored to French control in 1817, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, and the French held it, despite occasional British interruptions, until 1954, when it was de facto incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. With Mahé, Yanaon, Chandernagor and Pondichéry it was integrated de jure in 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Karaikal is known for Karaikal Ammaiyar, a Shaivite saint and Bhakti poetess among the sixty-three nayanmar, who lived there around the 5th century CE. Her temple holds each summer the Mangani, the Mango Festival, the town's main feast, and shares the Chandra Theertham tank with the Kailasanathar and Nithyakalyana Perumal temples. A dargah formed after the death in 1829 of Mastan Saheb, a Bukharan Sufi cleric, and a procession known as Kanduri is held there each year. Agriculture and fishing are the district's main sources of income, along its 26 kilometres of coast. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 200,222 people in the district.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karaikal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Further settlements followed at Chandernagore in Bengal, Mahe on the Malabar coast, <b>Karaikal</b> and Yanam.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-india">French India</a></li></ul></section>
